So the fuzz target feeds the input as SSML text. The input is a raw text file. The vulnerability is triggered by a malformed tag like `<foo`.

Let me now carefully analyze the `GetSsmlAttribute` function for the OOB read. Let me reconsider the `pw[-1]` access.

The key: `pw` is `px = &xml_buf[ix]`. When `ix == 0`, `pw[-1]` reads `xml_buf[-1]` which is OOB.

When is `ix == 0`? In `ProcessSsmlTag`:
```c
for (ix = 0; ix < (sizeof(tag_name)-1); ix++) {
        if (((c = xml_buf[ix]) == 0) || iswspace(c))
                break;
        tag_name[ix] = tolower((char)c);
}
```

`ix == 0` if `xml_buf[0]` is 0 or whitespace. But xml_buf[0] is the first char after `<`, which must be `/`, alpha, `!`, or `?`. So xml_buf[0] is not whitespace or 0. So `ix >= 1`.

Hmm, so `pw[-1]` reads xml_buf[ix-1] which is in bounds. So that's not the OOB.

Wait, let me reconsider. Maybe the OOB is in the `while (*pw == name[ix])` loop. Let me re-examine:

```c
while (*pw != 0) {
        if (iswspace(pw[-1])) {
                ix = 0;
                while (*pw == name[ix]) {
                        pw++;
                        ix++;
                }
                if (name[ix] == 0) {
                        // found the attribute
                        ...
                }
        }
        pw++;
}
```

The inner loop `while (*pw == name[ix])` reads `*pw` and `name[ix]`. If `*pw` matches `name[ix]` all the way to the end of `name` (where `name[ix] == 0`), then the loop continues only if `*pw == 0`. But `*pw == 0` means end of input string. So the loop would read `name[ix]` beyond its null terminator? No - if `name[ix] == 0` and `*pw == 0`, the loop continues, incrementing `ix` and reading `name[ix]` which is past the end of `name`. But `name` is a constant string literal, so reading past it is technically OOB but on a constant, not attacker-controlled.

Hmm, but that's not "read past the end of the string" referring to the input.

Let me reconsider. Let me look at the actual CVE. This is likely CVE-2018-... Let me search for the espeak-ng SSML vulnerability.

Actually, let me reconsider the whole thing. The description says "A malformed tag like `<foo`". Let me think about what happens with `<foo` where there's no closing `>`.

In readclause.c, the tag reading loop:
```c
while (!Eof() && (c1 != '>') && (n_xml_buf < N_XML_BUF)) {
        xml_buf[n_xml_buf++] = c1;
        c1 = GetC();
}
xml_buf[n_xml_buf] = 0;
```

For `<foo` at end of input, xml_buf = "foo". Then `ProcessSsmlTag("foo", ...)`.

In `ProcessSsmlTag`, `tag_name = "foo"`, `px = &xml_buf[3]` points to the null terminator.

Then for the tag type, `LookupMnem(ssmltags, "foo")` returns... "foo" is not in the table, so it returns the last value which is `mtab[ix].value` where mtab[ix].mnem == NULL. Looking at ssmltags, the last entry is `{ NULL, 0 }`, so it returns 0. So `tag_type = 0`.

Wait, `LookupMnem` - let me check. Actually `tag_type = LookupMnem(ssmltags, tag_name)`. If not found, returns the value of the NULL entry, which is 0. So `tag_type = 0`.

Then `switch (tag_type)` - case 0 doesn't match any case (SSML_STYLE=1, etc.). Actually let me check the enum values.

Let me look at ssml.h for the enum values.

